  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Show that $P(x)=x^2$ can be expressed in the form $a(x-1)^2+b(x-2)^2+c(x-3)^2$ for suitable values of $a$, $b$ and $c$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=3$, $b=-3$, $c=1$.\n\nExpand and equate the coefficients of $x^2$, $x$ and the constant term.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a(x-1)^2+b(x-2)^2+c(x-3)^2&=(a+b+c)x^2-2(a+2b+3c)x+a+4b+9c\\end{align*}$$\n\nEquating coefficients with $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a+b+c&=1\\\\a+2b+3c&=0\\\\a+4b+9c&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second, and the second from the third:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}b+2c&=-1\\\\2b+6c&=0\\\\b+3c&=0\\\\c&=1\\\\b&=-3\\\\a&=3\\end{align*}$$\n\nCheck:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3(x-1)^2-3(x-2)^2+(x-3)^2&=3x^2-6x+3-3x^2+12x-12+x^2-6x+9\\\\3(x-1)^2-3(x-2)^2+(x-3)^2&=x^2\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 78
  },
